<section class="space-y-6 leading-relaxed">
  <h2 class="mt-10 mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold">
    The Polarity of Team Development
  </h2>

  <p>
    A team constantly moves within a social-psychological tension field. On one
    side is the need for connection, orientation, and belonging. On the other
    side is the striving for individuality, distinction, and personal
    expression.
  </p>

  <p>
    Successful team development does not mean resolving these poles. It means
    finding a <strong>productive way to work with both</strong>. It creates
    space for integration <em>and</em> differentiation.
  </p>

  <div class="card-bordered my-8">
    <h3 class="mb-2 font-semibold">How the game reflects this</h3>
    <p class="text-sm opacity-80">
      Levels 1–3 focus heavily on integration: shared goals, team charter,
      understanding each other. Levels 4–6 introduce differentiation: strengths,
      roles, healthy competition. The game's structure embodies the principle of
      "integration before differentiation."
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="mt-10 mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold">
    Integration: What Holds the Team Together
  </h2>

  <p>
    Integration is the connective tissue of a team. It includes shared rules,
    common values, and collective goals. Integration creates safety and a sense
    of "we." It is the foundation without which difference is quickly
    experienced as a threat.
  </p>

  <p>
    When team members feel connected, they can handle disagreement, give honest
    feedback, and challenge each other without damaging relationships.
    Integration creates the psychological safety needed for productive conflict.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold">
    Differentiation: What Makes the Team Stronger
  </h2>

  <p>
    Differentiation is the counter-movement. It is the space for individuality,
    unique strengths, different perspectives, and personal expression. When
    integration is solid, differentiation becomes a source of:
  </p>

  <ul class="ml-6 list-disc space-y-2 opacity-80">
    <li><strong>Creativity</strong> — different viewpoints spark new ideas</li>
    <li><strong>Ownership</strong> — people can contribute in their own way</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Growth</strong> — productive friction pushes the team forward
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Without differentiation, a team becomes a hive mind — harmonious but
    stagnant, incapable of critical thinking or innovation.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold">
    Integration Before Differentiation
  </h2>

  <p>
    The sequence matters. A team must have enough shared ground before it can
    productively explore its differences. This is a developmental principle:
    before you can distinguish yourselves from each other, you must feel
    connected to each other.
  </p>

  <p>
    Rushing to differentiation without sufficient integration often leads to
    misunderstanding, conflict, or withdrawal. Team members may feel judged,
    unheard, or unsafe. The game's levels follow this logic: early levels build
    connection; later levels create space for difference.
  </p>

  <div class="border-warning/30 my-8 rounded-xl border p-6">
    <h3 class="text-warning mb-2 font-semibold">A note on complexity</h3>
    <p class="text-sm opacity-80">
      The cause-effect relationship described here — integration leading to
      shared understanding, differentiation leading to stronger relationships —
      is a simplification. Real team dynamics are multi-layered and complex.
      This framing is a deliberate choice: it provides an actionable starting
      point without getting lost in systemic complexity.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
